<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=abo-blood-group-system">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">ABO blood group system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ABO blood types</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ABO blood group system classifies human blood by the presence of the A and B antigens on red blood cells; the Austrian physician Karl Landsteiner discovered it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, dividing blood into three groups in 1901 after finding that some people's serum clumped others' red cells. His students found a fourth group, later named AB, the following year. It is the most important blood group system for transfusion, since a mismatch can cause a potentially fatal reaction. Landsteiner won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> for it in 1930.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abo-blood-group-system.jpg" alt="ABO blood group system" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fadi · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BlutAgglutination.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
